    },
    {
      "texts": [
        {
          "value": "The Gly-rich region is probably involved in binding calcium, which is required for target cell-binding or cytolytic activity"
        }
      ],
      "commentType": "DOMAIN"
    },
    {
      "texts": [
        {
          "evidences": [
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000305",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "2121650"
            }
          ],
          "value": "The three transmembrane domains are involved in pore formation by the cytotoxin"
        }
      ],
      "commentType": "DOMAIN"
    },
    {
      "texts": [
        {
          "evidences": [
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "10978310"
            },
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "12598051"
            },
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "32461253"
            },
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "7801126"
            },
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000269",
              "source": "PubMed",
              "id": "8808931"
            }
          ],
          "value": "Myristoylated by HlyC; the toxin only becomes active when modified (PubMed:10978310, PubMed:32461253, PubMed:7801126, PubMed:8808931). Mainly myristoylated, while a minor fraction is acylated with pentadecanoyl (C15:0; 26%) and heptadecanoyl (C17:0; 6%) fatty acyl groups (PubMed:10978310). Fatty acylation is involved in binding to host membranes and promotes the irreversible insertion of Hemolysin into the host cell membrane (PubMed:12598051). Can be activated by both myristoylation and palmitoylation, but HlyC catalyzes lysine myristoylation (PubMed:32461253)"
        }
      ],
      "commentType": "PTM"
    },
    {
      "texts": [
        {
          "evidences": [
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000305"
            }
          ],
          "value": "The hemolysin of E.coli is produced predominantly by strains causing extraintestinal infections, such as those of the urinary tract"
        }
      ],
      "commentType": "MISCELLANEOUS"
    },
    {
      "texts": [
        {
          "evidences": [
            {
              "evidenceCode": "ECO:0000305"
            }
          ],
          "value": "Belongs to the RTX prokaryotic toxin (TC 1.C.11) family"
        }
      ],
      "commentType": "SIMILARITY"
    }
